      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists argue that the text's historical context shows a right to keep and bear arms for self-defense against tyranny, not a blanket right to carry any weapon without restriction. They emphasize that the Founders were concerned with militia service and resisting government overreach, not modern recreational shooting. Living constitutionalists contend that the right to self-defense is a fundamental, evolving human right that must be interpreted to protect citizens from modern threats, including mass violence. They see the amendment as a shield against governmental infringement on personal liberty, which requires adapting to current societal dangers.",
